---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/results.json';

const { meta } = data as any;
const d = meta.device ?? {};
---

<Base
  title="device"
  path="/device/"
  lede="What the grid runs on, and one thing worth knowing about the timings."
>
  <section class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">what we're using</div></div>
    <p class="note">
      Everything runs locally on a <strong>{d.cpu ?? 'Apple silicon'}</strong> Mac
      ({d.memory}, {d.os}). Two harnesses drive the models: <strong>Claude Code</strong>
      {d.claude_cli && <> {d.claude_cli}</>} for Opus 5 and Fable 5, and
      <strong> Codex CLI</strong>{d.codex_cli && <> {d.codex_cli}</>} on the
      <code>{d.codex_profile}</code> profile for the GPT models. Video work is
      ffmpeg {d.ffmpeg}; the generator is Python {d.python}; the benchmark
      orchestrator is Rust {d.rust}.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Each run gets its own directory under <code>{d.run_root}</code> holding
      nothing but <code>clip.mp4</code>, with a {d.timeout_s
        ? `${Math.round(d.timeout_s / 60)}-minute`
        : '30-minute'} ceiling and no cap on turns or tokens. How to approach the
      video is entirely the model's choice.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      One caveat on that ceiling for this grid: it killed the agent process but
      not the ffmpeg and python children it had spawned, and those inherit the
      output pipe — so a few runs kept going well past the limit, the longest
      reaching 52 minutes. The harness now signals the whole process group, but
      that landed after this grid started, so treat the ceiling as soft here.
      Actual durations are recorded per run either way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">about the timings</div></div>
    <p class="note">
      The accuracy pass runs {d.concurrency ?? 8} agents at once, all of them
      driving ffmpeg and Python over 1800-frame video. Wall-clock under that load
      measures contention as much as it measures the model, so treat the
      <em>avg t</em> column as a rough shape rather than model speed. Every row
      records its concurrent-run count and the machine's load average, and clean
      timings come from a separate sequential pass instead of being quietly mixed
      into the same average.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
